<p class="lead">Ask what landscape lighting costs and the honest answer is: it depends on the plan. Two homes on the same street in Spring can end up with very different numbers because one needs six fixtures to look finished and the other needs thirty. Price follows the design, not the other way around. This guide walks through the factors that actually move a quote up or down, how to budget in phases, and what the system will cost to run once it is in the ground.</p>

<h2>The short answer</h2>
<p>There is no honest flat price for "landscape lighting." National cost guides commonly report professionally installed low-voltage systems landing somewhere in the range of a few hundred dollars per fixture, installed, with wide variation depending on fixture quality, labor, site conditions and region. Treat any number like that as a rough orientation, not a quote. The figure that matters is the one built around your property, your goals and the materials chosen.</p>
<p>At Spring Landscape Lighting we do not publish package prices, because a package assumes every yard needs the same thing. What we can do is explain where the money goes, so you can read any proposal with a clear eye.</p>

<h2>What drives the price</h2>
<p>Most of the cost in a professional system comes from a handful of decisions. Some are fixed by your property. Others are choices you control.</p>

<h3>Fixture count</h3>
<p>Every fixture carries its own hardware, connection and aiming time, so fixture count is the biggest lever on cost. A well-placed wash light with the right beam spread can cover a wide brick wall that a poorly planned system would hit with three narrow spots. More fixtures do not automatically make a better result. Often they make a worse one.</p>

<h3>Fixture quality and materials</h3>
<p>Solid brass and copper fixtures cost more up front than powder-coated aluminum, but they tend to hold up far better in Gulf Coast humidity, irrigation overspray and acidic soil. Brass weathers to a dark patina instead of corroding. Many aluminum fixtures look fine on day one and start to chalk, pit or bubble within a few seasons. Composite fixtures sit in between. Lens quality, gaskets and the socket or LED board inside also separate a fixture that lasts from one that fills with water after the first tropical storm.</p>

<h3>Integrated LED vs. lamp-based fixtures</h3>
<p>Integrated LED fixtures have the light source built in. They are typically sealed well and efficient, but when the LED eventually fails the whole fixture or module is replaced. Lamp-based fixtures accept a replaceable LED lamp (often an MR16 or similar), which can make future repairs and color or beam changes simpler. Neither is universally better. The right choice depends on how much flexibility you want and how the fixture is built.</p>

<h3>Wire runs and trenching</h3>
<p>Low-voltage cable is buried, usually a few inches below grade. Long runs to a back fence line, crossings under sidewalks or driveways, and dense root zones under mature oaks all add labor. Long runs may also need heavier wire gauge to control voltage drop, which adds material cost. A compact front bed near the transformer is far simpler than lighting a deep lot.</p>

<h3>Transformer</h3>
<p>The transformer converts 120V household power to roughly 12V for the fixtures. A quality multi-tap stainless transformer costs more than a big-box unit, but it lets the installer compensate for voltage drop on longer runs and usually lasts much longer outdoors. Size matters too: a good rule is to load a transformer to no more than about 80% of its rated capacity. Our <a href="/landscape-lighting-transformer-sizing/">transformer sizing guide</a> explains why.</p>

<h3>Controls</h3>
<p>A basic photocell and timer is inexpensive. Wi-Fi or app-based <a href="/services/smart-lighting-controls/">smart lighting controls</a> with multiple zones, astronomical scheduling and dimming add cost, but they also let you run the front facade all evening while the backyard turns off at 10 p.m.</p>

<h3>Design complexity and tree-mounted fixtures</h3>
<p>Uplighting a single crape myrtle is quick. Moonlighting a mature live oak with fixtures mounted 25 or 30 feet up the canopy requires ladders or lifts, careful wire routing up the trunk and more time. Properties with pools, multiple patios, long driveways and layered gardens simply take longer to design and install well.</p>

<h3>Repairs vs. new installation</h3>
<p>Repairing or upgrading an existing system is often less expensive than starting over, provided the wire and transformer are sound. Many older halogen systems in North Houston can be converted to LED. Others have corroded splices and undersized wire that make a rebuild more sensible. Our <a href="/services/landscape-lighting-repair/">landscape lighting repair</a> team can tell you which situation you are in.</p>

