<h2>How to budget in phases</h2>
<p>A lighting system does not have to be installed all at once. A phased plan often produces a better result than a rushed, compromised one, as long as the infrastructure is planned for the finished design from day one.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Design the whole property first.</strong> Even if you install only part of it, map every future fixture so wire routes and transformer capacity are planned correctly.</li>
<li><strong>Size the transformer for the end state.</strong> Installing a transformer large enough for later phases avoids replacing it in two years.</li>
<li><strong>Start where you look most.</strong> For most homes that is the front facade, entry and the trees seen from the street.</li>
<li><strong>Add safety and movement next.</strong> Paths, steps and the driveway apron make the property easier to use after dark.</li>
<li><strong>Finish with outdoor living.</strong> Patio, pool and backyard garden lighting can follow when the budget allows.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><strong>Tip:</strong> Ask for spare wire leads or a stubbed-out run where you expect future fixtures. Running wire while beds are open is far cheaper than trenching through mature plantings later.</div>

<h2>DIY vs. professional installation</h2>
<p>DIY kits are tempting, and for a short path along a single bed they can work. The trade-offs show up over time. Kit fixtures are often lighter-gauge aluminum or plastic, connections are typically pierce-style clips that corrode in wet soil, and kit transformers rarely offer multiple voltage taps. The bigger difference is design: knowing which beam spread to use, where to place a fixture so it lights the tree rather than the neighbor's window, and when to leave an area dark.</p>
<p>Professional installation costs more up front. In return you get sealed connections, correctly sized wire and transformer, and deliberate aiming that holds up for years.</p>

