<h2>What it costs to run</h2>
<p>Operating cost is the pleasant surprise. A modern LED system draws far less power than the halogen systems many Spring homes still have. A typical LED landscape fixture uses roughly 2 to 12 watts, versus 20 to 50 watts for halogen. Twenty 5-watt LEDs running six hours a night use about 18 kWh per month, which at common Texas residential rates works out to a few dollars.</p>
<p>For the full math, read <a href="/electricity-landscape-lighting-use/">how much electricity landscape lighting uses</a>, or plug your own numbers into the <a href="/tools/energy-cost-calculator/">energy cost calculator</a>. If you are weighing an upgrade, the <a href="/tools/led-savings-calculator/">LED savings calculator</a> compares halogen and LED side by side.</p>

<h2>Getting a quote you can compare</h2>
<p>When you compare proposals, look past the total. Ask how many fixtures are included and why, what the fixtures are made of, what transformer is specified and at what load, how splices are made, and what controls are included. A lower number with aluminum fixtures and a maxed-out transformer is not the same system as a higher one built to last. Our <a href="/our-process/">design and installation process</a> explains what we include, and you can <a href="/quote/">request a quote</a> whenever you are ready.</p>

